# THE SECOND ENVIRONMENT ON ONE HOST. Six variables and one network fix.
#
# ── A CORRECTION, BECAUSE THIS FILE SAID SOMETHING THAT WAS NOT TRUE ──────────
#
# This header used to read "Six variables, no second copy of anything", and item
# 2 below asserted the gateway files were made to follow `CF_NET_PREFIX`. Both
# were true of the CONSUMERS and false of the PRODUCER.
# `docker-compose.telemetry.yml` — the file that actually CREATES `edge`, `app`
# and `vault`, as `docker-compose.gateway.yml` says where it declares them
# external — hardcoded `cf-micro-edge`, `cf-micro-app` and `cf-micro-vault`, and
# named the LEGACY, STOPPED `stack_default` as the estate network.
#
# Measured, not inferred: with `CF_NET_PREFIX=cf-testnet CF_PROJECT=cf-testnet`
# set, `docker compose -f docker-compose.telemetry.yml config` emitted
# `cf-micro-app`, `cf-micro-edge` and `stack_default` — byte-identical to the
# unset run. So the six variables moved the testnet gateway to
# `cf-testnet-edge`/`-app` and left NOTHING able to create them, and the second
# environment could not start. That file is now parameterised; see its `networks:`
# block for the whole argument.
#
#
#   docker compose --env-file compose/testnet.env \
#     -f compose/docker-compose.estate.yml up -d
#
# ── WHAT THIS REPLACES, AND WHY IT IS NOT A SECOND SET OF COMPOSE FILES ───────
#
# `.env.example` said, of COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME: "Change only if you run two
# copies of this stack on one host, WHICH NOTHING HERE IS DESIGNED FOR." That was
# accurate, and three specific things made it so. All three are now variables
# with their existing values as defaults, so an unset environment renders these
# files byte-for-byte identically to before — verified with `docker compose
# config` against `git show HEAD:` for all three files, not assumed:
#
#   1. `name: cloudsforge-estate` was a LITERAL at docker-compose.estate.yml:64.
#      A top-level `name:` overrides COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME entirely, so setting
#      that variable did nothing at all — the documented escape hatch was already
#      closed by a line 6000 characters further down the same file. It is now
#      `${CF_PROJECT:-cloudsforge-estate}`.
#
#   2. The gateway files attach to `cf-micro-edge`, `cf-micro-app`,
#      `cf-micro-vault` and `cloudsforge-estate_default` by LITERAL NAME with
#      `external: true`. Two projects would therefore have shared one set of
#      networks — which is not isolation, it is one estate with two names, and
#      every service in each would have resolved the other's by container alias.
#      The gateway files now interpolate `${CF_NET_PREFIX:-cf-micro}` — and, as
#      the correction above records, `docker-compose.telemetry.yml` did NOT until
#      it was fixed alongside this note. It is the one that creates them.
#
#   3. Forty-five loopback debug ports (`127.0.0.1:41xx:4000`), the gateway's
#      443, and 9095/9096/9097 are all fixed. Only one process can bind a port;
#      the second project simply fails to start, which is at least loud.
#
# Volumes needed no change: compose namespaces them by project name, so
# `custody-keys` becomes `cf-testnet_custody-keys` automatically. That matters
# more than it looks — `custody-keys` holds every key the platform custodies, and
# two environments sharing it would be two ledgers spending one set of coins.
#
# ── WHY NOT KUBERNETES, IN ONE PARAGRAPH ──────────────────────────────────────
# The full argument is in micro-docs `ecosystem/26-public-deployment.md`. The
# short form: isolation here is a compose project, and the six lines below are
# the whole mechanism. A single-node k3s would add an orchestrator to get the
# same two-namespace separation, and `deploy.replicas` is already illegal for
# anything this gateway routes by DNS name.

# The compose project. Sets container names, the default network
# (`cf-testnet_default`) and every volume prefix in one go.
CF_PROJECT=cf-testnet

# The three external networks the gateway attaches to. `external: true` means
# compose attaches and never creates, so this must match whatever brought them up
# for THIS project.
#
# WHAT BRINGS THEM UP IS `docker-compose.telemetry.yml`, NOT THE ESTATE. That
# matters here more than it looks, because the telemetry plane is deliberately
# SINGLE (see the section at the end of this file) — one Grafana showing both
# environments. A single telemetry project can only create ONE prefix's networks.
#
# So setting this variable is necessary and NOT sufficient, and the missing half
# is stated here rather than discovered: `cf-testnet-edge` and `cf-testnet-app`
# have to be created before the testnet gateway starts, and the shared telemetry
# containers have to be attached to them or Prometheus cannot scrape the testnet
# gateway at all. Creating them is one command and it is in the runbook beside
# this file; what must not happen is a second telemetry stack, which is the thing
# this file's last section spends a page arguing against.
CF_NET_PREFIX=cf-testnet

# The leading digit of the 45 loopback debug ports: 4100-4144 becomes 5100-5144.
# One character rather than 45 overrides, because a 45-entry override list is the
# hand-maintained list this repository has paid for four times.
#
# These are debug ports and nothing routes through them — the gateway reaches
# every service over the docker network by name. They exist so an operator can
# curl one service without going through TLS.
CF_PORT_BASE=5

# The gateway's browser port. Mainnet keeps 443; testnet takes 10443, chosen so
# it reads as its original with a `1` in front. Both are bound to 127.0.0.1
# ONLY — public exposure is the tunnel's job, and that stays true here.
CF_GATEWAY_PORT=10443

# 9095/9096/9097 (http, https-alt, traefik ping) become 9195/9196/9197.
CF_GW_PORT_BASE=91

# The gateway's apex. `env/traefik.testnet.env` sets CF_WEB_APEX and CF_API_HOST
# to testnet.cloudsforge.online; see that file's header for the verification that
# a `testnet.` prefix needs no code change, and for the one way it could stop
# being true (`scripts/check-apex-prefix.py` fails on it).
CF_TRAEFIK_ENV=traefik.testnet

# ── NOT SET HERE, DELIBERATELY: the telemetry plane ───────────────────────────
#
# There is ONE observability stack for both environments, not two. It stays in
# the `cfmicro` project on its existing ports, and both estates ship to it.
#
# The argument: Prometheus, Grafana, Loki and Tempo cost ~770 MiB and buy nothing
# when duplicated — you want ONE Grafana showing both environments side by side,
# with an `env` label to tell them apart, because the question an operator
# actually asks during an incident is "is this happening on both?". Two Grafanas
# cannot answer it. Duplicating them would add ~770 MiB to a box being specified
# at 32 GB for other reasons.
#
# The cost, stated: a scrape config that fails to distinguish the two
# environments merges their metrics, and a merged EMBER supply figure across a
# real chain and a test one is exactly the kind of number that ends up in a
# reconciliation alarm. The `env` label is therefore load-bearing and belongs in
# prometheus/targets/, not here.
